.TH CANDLE-PROTOCOL 7 "November 2026" "wincan" "Miscellaneous Information Manual"
.SH NAME
candle\-protocol \- the HTTP protocol between a candle window and a candle supply
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR wincan (1)
shows candles that a candle supply serves over HTTP, as JSON.
.BR candled (8)
is such a supply; anything that speaks this protocol can be one (a script over an exchange's feed, say), and wincan needs nothing else from it.
.SH TRANSPORT
HTTP/1.1 with keep\-alive, GET only; a client polls about once a second.  The connection is either
.B https
(TLS 1.2 or later), in which the client shows a certificate of its own and is let in by the SHA\-256 fingerprint of that certificate, while the client pins the fingerprint of the server's self\-signed certificate (see
.BR candled (8),
SECURITY), or plain
.B http
on a loopback address.  A supply that is open to a network should be the first kind.
.SH REQUESTS
Only GET.  Answers are JSON; errors are an object with an
.B error
member.
.TP
.B /pairs
the names of the usable files written in the last three days, the most recently written first: \fB["BTC","ETH",...]\fR.
.TP
.B /candles?pair=BTC&tf=5m&n=60
the last
.I n
bars (at most 500; a gap in the data leaves no bar) of a timeframe, oldest first, as
.RS
.nf
{"pair", "tf", "price", "age_ms", "complete", "n",
 "t":[...], "o":[...], "h":[...], "l":[...], "c":[...], "v":[...]}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B t
is the start of each bar in seconds since the epoch;
.B price
is the last close;
.B age_ms
is the time since the collector's last candle (a collector that has stopped shows a growing age);
.B complete
is false while the older history is still being read.
.B sig
is a signature of the last two bars.  A client that polls sends it back as
.BR sig=... ,
with a small
.BR n ,
usually 2: if nothing has changed (as in a second without a trade) the answer is only
.B {"unchanged":true,"sig":...,"price":...,"age_ms":...,"complete":...}\fR,
otherwise the last
.I n
bars, to be merged by their start times into what the client already has.
.TP
.B /range?pair=ZEC&since=1700000000
the highest and lowest price since a time (Unix seconds):
.B {"pair",\:"since",\:"n",\:"high",\:"high_t",\:"low",\:"low_t",\:"price"}.
.TP
.B /heat?pair=ZEC
the modelled liquidation ladder of a pair, one bucket per percentage point from the price (0 to 1 per cent up to 19 to 20), longs below the price, shorts above:
.RS
.nf
{"pair", "sym", "src", "age_s", "ladder_age_s", "book_age_s", "mark", "price",
 "oi_usd", "bid_cov", "ask_cov", "cog", "cogn",
 "L":[20], "S":[20], "pl":[20], "ps":[20]}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B L
and
.B S
are the liquidation notional of each bucket (longs, shorts),
.B pl
and
.B ps
the pressure (those liquidations over the order\-book depth to the same distance),
.B cog
the centre of gravity as a per cent from the price, the mass\-weighted mean, and
.B cogn
the same with each level weighted by mass over distance squared.  An answer of 404 means no data for the pair, 503 that heat is not configured; wincan then shows no strip.
.SH ERRORS
400 for a request that is wrong, 404 for an unknown pair, 405 for anything but GET, 503 when a source is not available.  The body is always an object with an
.B error
member.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
curl \-\-cacert server.crt \-\-cert client.crt \-\-key client.key 'https://localhost:8766/candles?pair=BTC&tf=1m&n=2'
curl 'http://127.0.0.1:8766/pairs'          # a supply in plain HTTP on the loopback address
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR candled (8),
.BR wincan (1),
.BR wincan (5)
